This review covers the **4 October 2026 export**, not the live queue. The desk keeps working;
refresh the export and rerun these views before using the counts for a new handoff.

The [original incident](/kb/incidents/4471) says “one issue, one fix queued.” Its own
rendered table shows two causes. One parser fix does not fix the booking form's hint.

## Count reports once, classify by tenant and channel

<CauseTable ticket={4471} />

The table deduplicates report IDs before filtering to ticket 4471. It looks up the tenant's
locale and booking-form version, and uses the platform's date-part order. It does not treat
browser locale, subject, or the intake tag as proof of cause. Export pages overlap; repeated
rows are not extra people or extra reports. The intake tags disagree with the cause logic in
both directions, as the table shows.

### Parser: shared parser reads month first

Day-first tenant dates are read month first by the shared parser. For example, R-41200
reports a 7 April reminder becoming 4 July. App and email reports, and form reports whose
hint agrees with the tenant's date order, belong to this group under the current logic.
Fix the parser to respect the tenant's locale and test ambiguous dates with both parts at
most 12, plus unambiguous dates. Keep this separate from the form-hint fix.

### Hint: booking-v3 tells day-first users to enter month first

The booking-v3 form displays MM/DD/YYYY while the form reads the date in the tenant's
locale order. This affects form reports from Alder Physio, Copper Studio and Pale Harbour.
For example, R-41230 describes 6 January becoming 1 June. These reports were merged into
4471 but have no separate ticket in the export. Match the hint to the actual input parser
and locale, and test the displayed hint and resulting date together.

### Five reports need a separate investigation

The table's footnote is a warning, not proof that a date swap explains every body.
R-41202 says 12 May became 11 May; R-41209 says June bookings are one day off;
R-41236 says the invite arrived before the booking; R-41344 reports different weekdays in
Lisbon and Toronto; R-41620 says 6 October showed as Monday. They remain included in the
parser group according to the supplied classifier; do not double-count them or silently
claim a confirmed timezone bug. Inspect original timestamps, timezone conversion and
booking/invite logs before deciding whether to split another cause. The export alone
cannot establish that root cause.

## Reports arrived before the merge spike

<QueueTimeline ticket={4471} />

The two series measure different events: receivedAt is when a report arrived; mergedAt is
when the desk filed it into 4471. Reports arrive from April onward. The large July merge
spike is a backlog being filed, not evidence that the bug began in July or that reports
suddenly rose then. Continued arrivals after July show the issue remained active in this
export; a merge is not a fix.

## Replace subject-only triage

The [accepted 2024 decision](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject) is historical and is
preserved unchanged. Its subject-only rule conflicts with the working explorer below.
The [replacement decision](/kb/decisions/0002-dedupe-by-cause) and
[updated triage instructions](/kb/triage/dedupe) require evidence of the same cause.

<DedupeExplorer />

Different subjects can describe the same cause; the same subject and tenant can cover
parser and hint problems on different channels. Unknown tenants cannot be classified and
must be investigated. Read the body as well: the five reports above show the limit of
relying on the classifier alone.

